\DOC LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a machine-state {eventually} goal past an inlined modular-subtraction
subroutine, moving to a fresh successor state and adding an assumption
describing the subtraction's result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} (constant {0} in all observed traces); the
second is a {string list} naming the destination and the two operand buffers of
the subtraction step being discharged. On a goal {A ?- t} whose conclusion
asserts {eventually} at some machine state, the tactic produces one subgoal in
which that state variable is replaced throughout the conclusion by a fresh
successor state. Every hypothesis mentioning the old state — the
program-counter equation, the code-loaded assertion, the register and
memory-contents equations, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state — is removed and re-asserted about the new state, with the
program counter advanced, the {MAYCHANGE} relation possibly extended by a
further memory component, and the memory equation for the destination buffer
now giving the newly computed value. All other assumptions are carried over
unchanged. One new assumption is added, stating that provided both operands are
below the modulus (named by the first string argument), the destination value
equals the difference of the operands reduced modulo that modulus.
{
   A ?- t
   ========================================  LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC n [dest; op1; op2]
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent hypotheses relocated to
the new state, plus the new subtraction hypothesis, and {t'} is {t} at the new
state.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour, on
goals of this one {eventually}-with-machine-state shape.

\ENDDOC
